## 1. Introduction
Electrochemical biosensors have received attention because of their low cost, simplicity, minimal sample preparation, sensitivity and short response time (Li et al., 2016). To achieve the high sensitivity of a “sandwich” type biosensor, an important component needs to be improved: “the recognition layer” (Zeng et al., 2015). Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) are a relatively new class of porous inorganic-organic materials with fascinating structures and intriguing properties, such as high specific surface areas, tunable pore sizes and exposed active sites, good thermal stability and uniform structured nanoscale cavities (Yu and Yan, 2013). In addition, the Fe-MIL- ${\cdot}88\mathrm{NH}_{2}$ (Fe-MOFs) not only inherited the above advantages but also exhibited high stability and good biocompatibility in aqueous solution (Liu et al., 2013). Thus, Fe-MOFs have drawn a high degree of attention as a template. To improve the sensitivity of the proposed biosensor and capture more streptavidin (SA), gold nanoparticles (AuNPs) were introduced to immobilize SA via Au- ${\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{2}}$ bonds. Herein, Fe-MOFs were used as the template to immobilize AuNPs and obtained the AuNPs@Fe-MOFs compounds. Compared with individual AuNPs, AuNPs@Fe-MOFs confer much higher selectivity and sensitivity to biosensor.
Meanwhile, “the signal transducer layer” was the other key component that needed to be improved (Zeng et al., 2015). Nanocomposites with excellent catalytic properties can greatly improve the sensitivity of detection. Cerium dioxide $\left(\mathrm{CeO}_{2}\right)$ nanoparticles have attained significant attention due to their unique catalysis properties, high adsorption capability and enhancement of the electrons transfer effect between the electrode and materials modified on the electrode surface (Li et al., 2017). Due to the above advantages, there have been extensive applications in the field of catalysis in the form of pure $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ or immobilization of metal components (Zhang et al., 2012). However, the prepared $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ usually forms aggregates that lead to a lower catalytic activity. To improve the stability of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ , carboxyl fullerene $(\mathrm{c}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{C}_{60})$ was introduced for its high stability and large surface area. In addition, $c\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C}_{60}$ shows better water dispersibility compared with fullerene. Thus, a simpler synthesis method and the application of non-toxic solvents have been achieved in the synthesis of $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/P t N P s}$ . Finally, $\mathbf{c-C_{60}}$ with large specific area can bind with more $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ nanoparticles to amplify the signal (Chen et al., 2016; Lu et al., 2014; Yu et al., 2016). Moreover, to bring the role of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ into full play for signal amplification, platinum nanoparticles (PtNPs) were chosen, binding with the signal probe, owing to its significantly electrocatalytic activity, adsorbing strongly to amino and thiol groups (Zhang et al., 2014). The synergistic catalysis of $\mathsf{C e O_{2}/P t N P s}$ achieves the signal amplification by catalysing $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . In summary, compared with individual CeO2 and PtNPs, $_{\mathrm{c-C60/CeO2/}}$ PtNPs composites have more efficient catalytic ability because of the synergistic catalysis of H2O2 by CeO2 and PtNPs and the large surface area of $\mathbf{c-C_{60}}$ . Thus, the combination of $c\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C}_{60}$ , $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ and PtNPs achieved signal amplification.
## 2. Experimental
## 2.1. Reagent and apparatus
## 2.2. Preparation of Fe-MIL-88 MOFs and AuNPs@Fe-MIL-88-MOFs
Fe-MIL-88 MOFs were synthesized according to the literature (Liu et al., 2013). AuNPs@Fe-MOFs were prepared by the $\mathrm{\DeltaNaBH_{4}}$ reduction method. Briefly, $0.126\mathrm{~g~}$ of 2-aminoterephthalic acid and $0.187\ g$ of $\mathrm{FeCl}_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ were dissolved in $15\mathrm{mL}$ of dimethylformamide (DMF). The mixture was kept in a silicone oil bath at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ under continuous magnetic stirring. After reacting for $15\mathrm{min}$ , $3.45\mathrm{mM}$ acetic acid was added to this solution. After slowly cooling to $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , the prepared Fe-MOFs were purified by centrifugation and washed with DMF, ethanol and ultrapure water to remove surplus reactants. Finally, the precipitate was dried in a vacuum oven overnight. The synthesis of AuNPs@Fe-MOFs was as follows: First, $1\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}O$ $(1.0\%$ , w/ v) was added to $1\mathrm{mL}$ of the prepared Fe-MOFs solution $(1\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.$ ) and vigorously sonicated for $15\mathrm{min}$ . Subsequently, $2\mathrm{mL}$ of a $\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ solution $(3.8\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ ) was dropwise added to the mixture with stirring for $30\mathrm{min}$ . Then, the mixture was purified by centrifugation and washed with Milli-Q water three times. Finally, the prepared products were dried at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ before use.
## 2.3. Preparation of $c–C_{6o}/C e O_{2}$
Previously method was used for the preparation of $\mathbf{c}{\mathbf{-}}{\mathbf{C_{60}/C e O_{2}}}$ composites with a little modification (Ujjain et al., 2014). First, $10\mathrm{mg}$ of $c\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C}_{60}$ was dissolved in $5\mathrm{mL}$ of ultrapure water under ultrasonication for $10\mathrm{min}$ at room temperature. Next, $10~\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{Ce}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{3}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ $(10.8\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ ) was mixed with $10\mathrm{mL}$ of hexamethylenetetramine $(3.5\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\mathrm{\Lambda}$ ). Then, both solutions were mixed and kept in a water bath at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{5\mathrm{h}}$ and washed with Milli-Q water several times. Subsequently, the prepared precipitate was dried at $60~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ before further use.
Subsequently, $20\mathrm{mg}$ of $\mathbf{c}{\mathbf{-}}{\mathbf{C_{60}/C e O_{2}}}$ composites were dispersed in ${\boldsymbol{5}}\mathrm{mL}$ of ethanol and sonicated for $15\mathrm{min}$ to obtain dispersive solution. Then, $0.1\mathrm{mL}$ of 3-aminopropyltriethoxysilane $(98\%)$ was mixed with the above solution and kept reflux at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $1.5\mathrm{h}$ . After cooling to room temperature, the obtained $\mathbf{c}{\mathbf{-}}{\mathbf{C}}_{60}/{\mathbf{C}}{\mathbf{e}}{\mathbf{O}}_{2}$ composites were washed with ultrapure water by centrifugation at $8000\mathrm{rpm}$ . Eventually, the prepared products were dried at $50^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ before further use.
2.4. Preparation of $c–C_{60}/C e O_{2}/P t N P s,$ PtNPs, $c-C_{6O}/P t N P s$ and $c\mathrm{-}C_{60}/$ $C e O_{2}/A u N P s$
The PtNPs supported on $\mathbf{c}{\mathbf{-}}{\mathbf{C_{60}/C e O_{2}}}$ were synthesized by the $\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ reduction method. Briefly, $1\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6.}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(1.0\%$ , w/v) was added to $1\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathbf{c}{\mathbf{-}}{\mathbf{C_{60}/C e O_{2}}}$ solution $(2\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ and vigorously sonicated for 5 min. Then, $2\mathrm{mL}$ of a $\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ solution $(3.8\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ )was added dropwise to the mixture with stirring for $30\mathrm{min}$ , followed by centrifuging, washing with Milli-Q water, and dissolving in $1\mathrm{mL}$ of Milli-Q water for further use. $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/A u N P s}$ and $\mathrm{c}{\mathrm{-}}\mathrm{C}_{60}\mathrm{/PtNPs}$ composites were synthesized by a similar method, except that $1\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{\mathrm{H}}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6}$ $(1\%)$ was displaced by $1\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{HAuCl}_{4}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}O$ $(1.0\%$ , w/v) and $1\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathbf{c}{\mathbf{-}}{\mathbf{C_{60}/C e O_{2}}}$ $(2\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ was displaced by $1\mathrm{mL}$ of $c\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C}_{60}$ $(2\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ . PtNPs were prepared as follows. First, $1\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{PtCl}_{6.}6\mathrm{H}_{2}0$ $(1.0\%$ , w/v) was added to $1\mathrm{mL}$ of Milli-Q water. Then, $2\mathrm{mL}$ of $\mathrm{NaBH}_{4}$ solution $(3.8\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}\mathrm{\Lambda}^{}$ )was added dropwise into the above solution with stirring for $30\mathrm{min}$ , followed by centrifuging and washing with Milli-Q water. The synthesized products were dissolved in $1\mathrm{mL}$ of Milli-Q water.
2.5. Preparation of $c-C_{60}/C e O_{2}/P t N P s/S P$ , $c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/A u N P s/S P_{3}$ , $c\mathrm{-}C_{60}/$ $P t N P s/S P$ and $P t N P s/S P$
In brief, $1\mathrm{mL}$ of as-prepared $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/P t N P s}$ were mixed with $200~\ensuremath{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of $1\upmu\mathrm{M}$ amino-modified signal probe (SP) and stirred gently at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $24\mathrm{h}$ . Afterward, $1\mathrm{mg}$ of BSA was added to $1\mathrm{mL}$ of TE buffer and the mixture was used to block the nonspecific binding sites of c$\mathrm{C}_{60}/\mathrm{CeO}_{2}/\mathrm{PtNPs}$ for another $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ . Then, $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/P t N P s/S P/B S A}$ bioconjugates were centrifuged at $8000\mathrm{rpm}$ and washed three times with Milli-Q water, followed by dispersal in $1\mathrm{mL}$ of hybridization solution for further use. The preparation process of $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/P t N P s/S P}$ is shown in Scheme 1A. The $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/A u N P s/S P}$ were synthesized by a similar method, except that $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/P t N P s}$ were displaced by $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/A u N P s}$ . Furthermore, $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/P t N P s/S P}$ and $\mathrm{PtNPs/SP}$ bioconjugates were also synthesized with an analogous process for $\mathbf{c-C_{60}}/$ $\mathrm{CeO_{2}/P t N P s/S P}$ , except that $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/P t N P s}$ were displaced by c${\mathrm{C}}_{60}/{\mathrm{PtNPs}}$ and PtNPs.
## 2.6. Fabrication of the sensing interfaces
Scheme 1 shows the preparation procedure of the biosensor. Prior to modification, GCE was successively polished with 0.3 and $0.05{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ alumina powders, and the electrode was then sequentially sonicated in Milli-Q water, alcohol and Milli-Q water for $5\mathrm{{min}}$ . Initially, $6{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of AuNPs@Fe-MOFs aqueous suspension $(2\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{mL}^{-1})$ was cast onto the electrode surface. After drying, $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of SA $(100\upmu\mathrm{g}\mathrm{mL}^{-1}.$ ) solution was coated onto the AuNPs@Fe-MOFs nanocomposite-modified electrode for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . After that, these electrodes were washed with Milli-Q water to remove unbound SA and dried in air. Then, $10\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of $1~{\upmu\mathrm{M}}$ bioCP was cast onto the GCE/AuNPs@Fe-MOFs and incubated at $4^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $^{12\mathrm{h}}$ . Then, the modified electrode was rinsed with washing buffer and placed into $6\upmu\mathrm{L}$ of BSA $(1\%$ , w/v) for $30\mathrm{min}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ to eliminate nonspecific adsorption.
## 2.7. Electrochemical measurements
Detection was based on the typical procedures of the sandwich reaction (Dong et al., 2012). The DNA biosensor (GCE/AuNPs@Fe-MOFs/ SA/bio-CP/BSA) was incubated with various concentrations of target DNA for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . The obtained $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/P t N P s/S P}$ was coated on GCE/AuNPs@Fe-MOFs/SA/bio-CP/BSA/target electrode for $^{2\mathrm{h}}$ at $37^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ . After the reaction, the electrode was washed with washing buffer to remove unbound conjugates. Ultimately, the electrochemical signal of the amperometric i-t curve was recorded at $0.4\mathrm{V}$ in ${\boldsymbol{5}}\mathrm{mL}$ of phosphate buffered saline (PBS). When the background current was stable, $20{\upmu\mathrm{L}}$ of $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ $\left(1.4\mathrm{mol}\mathrm{L}^{-1}\right).$ ) was added to the PBS solution, and then the current changes were recorded. Current0 is the blank specimen current change and Current is the specimen current change: ΔCurrent $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ Current - Current0.
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Characteristics of the AuNPs@Fe-MOFs composites
FE-SEM was used to characterize the size and morphology of the asprepared nanomaterials. As shown in Fig. S1A, most of the Fe-MOFs had a regular octahedron structure. The average diameter of Fe-MOFs was approximately $200\mathrm{nm}$ , which was in accordance with the previous report (Liu et al., 2013). After AuNPs were in situ reduced on the surface of Fe-MOFs, a large number of bright dots spread along the FeMOFs were observed, as distinguished from Fe-MOFs, as shown in Fig. S1B. A further demonstration of the formation of the AuNPs@Fe-MOFs composites by EDS and $\zeta$ -potential were displayed. As shown in Fig. S1C, there were significant peaks corresponding to C, N, O, Au and Fe in the EDS image of AuNPs@Fe-MOFs. The $\zeta$ -potential of Fe-MOFs and AuNPs@Fe-MOFs were 20.5 and 15.3, respectively (Fig. S1D). These results further indicated the successful modification of Fe-MOFs by the negatively charged AuNPs (Wang et al., 2017). These results revealed that AuNPs@Fe-MOFs were successfully synthesized.
## 3.2. Characterization of the $c–C_{6o}/C e O_{2}$ /PtNPs composites
To verify the successful formation of the signal materials, FE-SEM and EDS were used. As shown in Fig. 1A, the $\mathsf{c}\mathsf{-C}_{60}$ showed a typically spherical shape and the average size of $\mathbf{c-C_{60}}$ was approximately $100\mathrm{nm}$ . Fig. 1B shows the typical structure of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ with an average diameter of approximately $20\mathrm{nm}$ , which was in accordance with previous reports (Ujjain et al., 2014). As shown in Fig. 1C, the as-obtained $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ were dispersed on the surface of $\mathsf{c}{\mathsf{-}}\mathsf{C}_{60}$ , the average diameter of the hybrid was approximately $150\mathrm{nm}$ . Meanwhile, there were significant peaks corresponding to C, O and Ce in the EDS image of $\mathbf{c}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C}_{60}/\$ $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ (Fig. 1E). From these results, it was confirmed that the $\mathbf{c}{\mathbf{-}}{\mathbf{C_{60}/C e O_{2}}}$ had been successfully synthesized. After PtNPs were in situ reduced on the surface of $\mathbf{c}{\mathbf{-}}{\mathbf{C}_{60}}/{\mathbf{CeO}_{2}}$ , a large number of bright dots spread along the $\mathbf{c}{\mathbf{-}}{\mathbf{C}}_{60}/{\mathbf{C}}{\mathbf{e}}{\mathbf{O}}_{2}$ were observed (Fig. 1D). EDS characterization of $\mathbf{c-C_{60}}/\$ $\mathrm{CeO_{2}/P t N P s}$ was also employed. As shown in Fig. 1F, several intense peaks of Pt were observed, suggesting that PtNPs were successfully modified on $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}}$ , proving the successful synthesis of $\mathbf{c-C_{60}}/$ $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}/\mathsf{P t N P s}$ . X-ray photoelectron spectroscopy (XPS) was used to further confirm the successful synthesis of $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/P t N P s}$ composites. As showed in Fig. 1G, the characteristic peaks for the Pt4f, C1s, O1s and Ce3d core level regions were clearly observed in the $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/P t N P s}$ composites. The composites showed the spectrum of C1s, O1s and Ce3d, verifying the successful synthesis of $\mathbf{c}{\mathbf{-}}{\mathbf{C_{60}/C e O_{2}}}$ . Moreover, the Pt4f core level suggested that the $\mathbf{c}{\mathbf{-}}{\mathbf{C_{60}/C e O_{2}}}$ effectively anchored PtNPs. The spectrum of Ce3d and Pt4f are displayed in Fig. 1H and I. These results revealed that $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/P t N P s}$ composites were successfully synthesized (Deng et al., 2014). In addition, a further testimony of the formation of the $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/P t N P s}$ through $\zeta$ -potential and FT-IR spectroscopy were displayed in S3.1.
Specific coupling among the target DNA, bio-CP and SP were investigated by agarose gel electrophoresis. In Fig. S3, lane a was 20 bp marker and bio-CP (lane b) exhibited the highest mobility due to its low molecular weight. The bio-CP-target DNA conjugates (lane d) showed lower mobility, compared with target DNA (lane c) and bio-CP. The above results verified the successful hybridization of bio-CP and target DNA. Further, compared with SP (lane e), lane f had the same volume of bio-CP and SP added and had no significant mobility change. However, the band intensity was weakened because SP cannot hybridize with bioCP. To verify the successful hybridization of SP and target DNA, lane g revealed that SP can hybridize with the target and have a lower mobility than lane e. Finally, after target DNA was hybridized with SP and bio-CP, lane h showed the lowest mobility, which confirmed that target DNA successfully hybridized with bio-CP and SP. These results testified the hybridization feasibility of our DNA biosensor.
## 3.3. The electrochemical behaviour of different nanomaterials for signal tags
The electrochemical behaviours of different nanomaterials were compared by amperometric i-t curves. As shown in Fig. 2A, curve a showed the i-t responses of $\mathsf{c}\mathsf{-C}_{60}$ , which verified $c\mathrm{-}\mathsf{C}_{60}$ has no catalytic ability with $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Compared with curve a, the current of curve b was increased. Thus, $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ (curve b) showed certain catalytic ability. This result indicated that $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ can catalyse $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , which is consistent with previous report (Ujjain et al., 2014). After $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ was linked with $\mathbf{c-C_{60}}{}_{;}$ , the composite of $\mathbf{c}{\mathbf{-}}{\mathbf{C_{60}/C e O_{2}}}$ (curve c) showed an enhanced catalysis ability compared with curves a and b owing to the large specific area of $\mathsf{c}\mathsf{-C}_{60}$ , which can increase the loading of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ and enhance the catalytic ability. The inset showed enlarged details of curves a, b and c. Finally, when PtNPs were assembled on the surface of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ , the current value was distinctly increased (curve e) due to the syncatalytic effect of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ and PtNPs. Moreover, curves d showed the electrochemical response of $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/A u N P s}$ , which indicated that PtNPs exhibited better catalytic ability than AuNPs. These results demonstrated that the proposed signal material used in this biosensor has the best catalytic ability compared with the other materials in this experiment.
## 3.4. Electrochemical characterization of the stepwise modified electrodes
The stepwise fabrication process of the biosensor was electrochemically characterized by CV and electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) in a $5\mathrm{mM}$ $\mathrm{[Fe(CN)_{6}]}^{3-/4-}$ solution. The results of CV were shown in Fig. 2B, the curve of the bare GCE (curve a) had a pair of typical reversible redox peaks. After the AuNPs@Fe-MOFs were coated onto the electrode, the peak current was obviously increased (curve b), because of the excellent electrical conductivity of AuNPs@Fe-MOFs. After SA was coated on the modified electrode, the peak current was decreased (curve c) owing to the poor conductivity of SA. When bio-CP was added, the peak current was further decreased (curve d) because of the negatively charged phosphate backbone of the capture probe could prevent the ferricyanide anions from approaching the electrode surface. After blocking with BSA, a further decrease was observed on the peak current (curve e), due to the non-conductivity of BSA hindering electron transfer.
## 3.5. The feasibility of signal-amplifcation strategies
To investigate the signal amplification of the proposed biosensor, the biosensor was utilized with three types $(\mathrm{PtNPs}/{\mathrm{SP}}$ , $\mathsf{c{-}C_{60}/P t N P s/S P}$ , and $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/A u N P s/S P)}$ of labeled probes with the same concentration ${\bf\Lambda}^{\mathrm{(100pM)}}$ of target DNA (Fig. 2). As shown in Fig. 2D, the current response of the biosensor with $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/P t N P s/S P}$ was improved in comparison with that of $\mathrm{PtNPs}/{\mathrm{SP}}$ . This improvement owes to the catalytic activity of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ and PtNPs towards $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Additionally, when the biosensor was incubated with $\mathrm{c}{\cdot}\mathrm{C}_{60}{/}\mathrm{PtNP}s{/}\mathrm{SP}$ (Fig. 2E), the current response was increased compared with that of $\mathrm{PtNPs/SP}$ . $\mathbf{c}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C}_{60}$ has a large surface area, which makes it a great nanocarrier and increased the loading amounts of PtNPs (Chen et al., 2016). Compared with $\mathrm{c{-}}\mathrm{C_{60}/C e O_{2}/P t N P s/S P}$ , the current response of $\mathsf{c{-}C_{60}/P t N P s/S P}$ (Fig. 2E) was lower. These results were attributed to the syncatalytic activity of $\mathsf{C e O}_{2}$ and PtNPs towards $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and indicated that the $\mathbf{c}\mathbf{-}\mathbf{C}_{60}/\$ $\mathrm{CeO_{2}/P t N P s}$ nanomaterial enhanced the catalytic ability towards $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ . Moreover, the current response was remarkably increased when the biosensor was incubated with $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/P t N P s/S P}$ compared with the $\mathrm{c{-}C_{60}/C e O_{2}/A u N P s/S P}$ (Fig. 2F) because the catalytic ability of PtNP is stronger than that of AuNP (Liu et al., 2014). Thus, we chose $\mathbf{c-C_{60}}/\$ $\mathrm{CeO_{2}}/\mathrm{PtNPs}/\mathrm{SP}$ as the signal tag throughout the experiment.
